00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.150
is, I come from,
again, Container World,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.730
and I'm used to using
something like Docker, where

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.890
I can declaratively
say, this is what

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:24.020
I want my image to look like.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.640
I want it to have this layer.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.230
I want it to have
this set of tooling.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.700
Is there anything like
that for VMs or Google VMs?

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.325
BRAIN DORSEY: Absolutely,
and in particular,

00:02:35.325 --> 00:02:36.950
people use a lot of
Terraform for that.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.720
But I want to take a
step back [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.200
some of the things people
do on VMs are manual.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.370
And for a long-lived
VM, a manual

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.180
setup and take some backups,
that is a reasonable thing

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.570
to do for a one-off.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.810
But as soon as you start
having multiple copies of that

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:00.770
or more complex setup, you
start wanting automation.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.010
So there are multiple
different tools

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.250
that use the same APIs to
create VMs and configure

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.910
the networking and the like.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.570
In particular, Terraform
is very, very common.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.370
I think it's also worth checking
out Pulumi, and both of those

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.350
will let you set
up a configuration,

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.990
declare how you want things
to look, and push that out.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.577
And part of the reason I
think this is so important--

00:03:23.577 --> 00:03:25.160
so it's important
for production to be

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.380
able to make things consistent.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.880
But in addition to that,
testing is really hard.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.580
Almost nobody can afford to run
a full test environment that's

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.320
the size of production.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.830
And in physical machines, it,
as far as I know, almost never

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.060
happens.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.250
But if you have
things automated,

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.250
you can run a test of a
smaller number of machines

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.800
or the same number of machines
for a shorter amount of time

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.780
for way less money.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:04:00.310
You're going to be talking 1%
or parts of a percent of what

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.710
it costs to run a production
and do a completely

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.570
valid test in the
same environment using

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:05.600
the same tools.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.090
So that's really powerful.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:09.040
CARTER MORGAN: Oh, wow,
that's really cool.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.830
And I've actually seen that
pattern a lot in Kubernetes.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.590
I talk about Kubernetes a lot
