00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.079
But in Kubernetes,
you have this ability

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:19.980
to automate what happens
when you do certain actions.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.029
So if I wanted to
release some code

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:23.680
and have it go to a
small staging area

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.260
before it went out fully to
production, you could do that.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.910
And what I'm hearing--

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:29.380
tell me if I'm
wrong here, but it

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.360
sounds like you can
do that same thing,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.805
but on a different scale.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:34.930
You might be able to do
that with your entire cloud

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.597
infrastructure using some of the
tooling you were talking about.

00:04:37.597 --> 00:04:39.347
BRAIN DORSEY: Yeah,
that's pretty much it.

00:04:39.347 --> 00:04:41.500
So people use things
like Terraform

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:46.510
to declare the setup
of things like--

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:48.970
including a Kubernetes
cluster itself, maybe

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.870
buckets for storage, maybe
a database, some VMs,

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:53.710
like I mentioned.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.890
And you've got your entire
setup all scripted out

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.282
and repeatable and ready to go.

00:04:58.282 --> 00:04:59.740
CARTER MORGAN: See,
that's amazing.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.323
And this is one of
the reasons why I'm

00:05:01.323 --> 00:05:02.980
happy that I've been convinced.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.060
Something I am
curious about, though,

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.370
is, if I already
had my own system--

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.760
maybe I switched over
to VMs from on prem.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.630
I have my own way of accessing
my workloads, my VMs,

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.730
